She ran east across the quiet hills, singing of rain on moss, fox kits curled together, milk warming by the hearth, bread divided at doorways, and careful hands mending bowls. The seed listened. Its heat softened; sparks fell behind her and opened into yellow flowers.

At the tallest cedar, Luma placed it among the clouds. It unfolded slowly.

Since then, dawn follows her tracks, remembering gentleness.
